1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:03,500
How do solar panels turn
sunlight into electricity?

2
00:00:03,580 --> 00:00:04,990
Let us take it step by step.

3
00:00:05,180 --> 00:00:09,300
A solar panel is made of many small
cells, usually built from material called

4
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:10,000
silicon.

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:14,280
Silicon is a semiconductor, which means
it can carry electricity under the right

6
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:15,050
conditions.

7
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:19,540
When sunlight hits a solar cell, tiny
particles of light called photons knock

8
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:22,140
electrons loose from the silicon atoms.

9
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:26,000
The cell is built with two layers
that push these free electrons in one

10
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:26,770
direction.

11
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:30,450
That steady flow of electrons
is an electric current.

12
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:34,440
Thin metal lines on the surface of the
cell collect this current and send it out

13
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:35,360
through wires.

14
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:36,420
But there is a catch.

15
00:00:36,620 --> 00:00:40,580
The electricity that comes out of a panel
is direct current, while most homes use

16
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:41,850
alternating current.

17
00:00:42,040 --> 00:00:45,980
So a box called an inverter converts it
into the kind of power your lights and

18
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:46,940
fridge can use.

19
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:51,640
On a sunny day, a home system can
produce more power than the family needs.

20
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:56,500
The extra power can charge a battery for
the evening or flow back into the grid for

21
00:00:56,500 --> 00:00:57,550
neighbors to use.

22
00:00:57,740 --> 00:01:01,800
Panels work best when they face the sun
and are kept free of dust and shade.

23
00:01:01,980 --> 00:01:05,460
Even on cloudy days, they still
produce some power just less.

24
00:01:05,660 --> 00:01:10,160
A typical panel keeps working for 25 years
or more, with very little maintenance.

25
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:15,220
Today, solar power is one of the cheapest
ways to produce electricity in many parts

26
00:01:15,220 --> 00:01:16,140
of the world.

27
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:21,420
From rooftops in busy cities to large
solar farms in the desert, the same simple

28
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:22,600
idea is at work.

29
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:27,140
Sunlight in, electricity out,
and no smoke along the way.
